<h2>The transaction and the suit</h2>

<p>By an Agreement to Sell dated 1 November 2007, the plaintiff agreed to purchase a 300 square yard property in Village Shalimar, Delhi for Rs 30 lakh, and paid Rs 4,15,000 in instalments between 2007 and 2011. The property was entangled in probate proceedings; Letters of Administration issued only in February 2011, and the seller received possession in May 2013. The buyer sent a legal notice in May 2013 and sued for specific performance in July 2013. The trial court refused specific performance for want of readiness and willingness but decreed refund of Rs 4,15,000 with 6% interest. Only the buyer appealed.</p>

<h2>What the High Court decided</h2>

<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Capacity unproved</h3><p>The only bank statement on record covered May–June 2013, after the suit was filed — confirming the absence of funds at the relevant time in 2010–2011. Specific performance was rightly refused.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Termination disproved</h3><p>The seller's plea that the buyer terminated the agreement in January 2012 had no evidentiary support; the trial court's acceptance of it was reversed.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>No forfeiture</h3><p>With not an iota of evidence of loss, the seller could not retain the Rs 4.15 lakh. Sections 73–74 of the Contract Act and the Kailash Nath principle governed.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>No windfall either</h3><p>The buyer's claim to double the amount failed for want of pleaded and proved loss; refund with 6% interest from 30 May 2013 till realisation was adequate.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The legal architecture</h2>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>Role in the case</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 16(c), Specific Relief Act</td><td>Bar on relief without proof of continuous readiness and willingness</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sections 20–22, Specific Relief Act</td><td>The discretionary and compensatory framework around specific performance (as it stood for this pre-2018 agreement)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 73, Contract Act</td><td>Compensation for loss actually suffered from breach</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 74, Contract Act</td><td>Reasonable compensation where a sum is named — no penalty, no windfall; forfeiture needs proof of loss</td></tr>
</table>

<p>A note on timing: for agreements after the Specific Relief (Amendment) Act, 2018, specific performance is the rule rather than a discretion, but Section 16(c) readiness and willingness survives untouched — so the evidentiary discipline this judgment applies remains fully current.</p>

<h2>Practical guidance</h2>

<p>Buyers who intend to enforce an agreement to sell should build the financial record as the transaction runs: bank balances or sanction letters contemporaneous with each stage, written calls on the seller to perform, and prompt protest when the seller delays. Litigation reconstructed years later from a single post-suit bank statement will not carry Section 16(c). Sellers, conversely, should not assume that a failed specific performance suit converts the advance into their money: forfeiture requires a contractual stipulation, reasonableness, and — for anything beyond nominal earnest — proof of actual loss, such as a documented resale at a lower price.</p>

<p>The judgment also illustrates a quiet point of appellate strategy: the seller did not appeal the refund decree, yet the buyer's appeal allowed the court to correct the termination finding while affirming the outcome. Parties should assess, at the first-appeal stage, which findings — not just which outcomes — they can live with.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This article summarises a Delhi High Court first appeal for general information. Specific performance litigation is document-driven and outcome depends on the complete record; nothing here is legal advice.</p></div>
